{
  "summary": "The root cause is that find_config() in config.py walks up the directory tree from cwd and falls back to the global ~/MAD/config.json when no local .mad/config.json is found. This means lock files (constructed via config.mad_dir) always resolve to ~/MAD/.mad/.pipeline-lock.tui regardless of which project started the service. When a second service starts from ~/chickentracks.app, it finds the same global config, tries to acquire the same lock file, and fails. The fix should ensure per-project isolation — either by (a) having the systemd service unit set an Environment=MAD_DIR={project_dir}/.mad variable (the runner already supports MAD_DIR) so Config() uses the correct project-local .mad directory, or (b) making the lock file path incorporate the project directory name/hash so different projects never collide, or (c) ensuring find_config() respects the WorkingDirectory set by the service and that ~/chickentracks.app has its own .mad/config.json created during 'pipeline service install'. Option (a) is the simplest and most correct — the systemd unit template in service.py line 50 already sets WorkingDirectory but doesn't set MAD_DIR, so adding one line fixes the issue with minimal blast radius."
}
